%I A398792 #11 Aug 23 2026 19:12:26
%S A398792 0,0,1,3,8,18,39,82,170,351,721,1476,3012,6130,12450,25241,51105,
%T A398792 103358,208840,421643,850737,1715546,3457791,6966495,14030369,
%U A398792 28247507,56854178,114400435,230136995,462857658,930718308,1871137623,3761106610,7558807251,15188796435,30516174184
%N A398792 Number of values 0 <= r < 2^n such that the trajectories of r and r+1 under the Collatz map f (A014682), satisfy f^(n)(r) = f^(n)(r+1) and contain the same number of odd terms among the first n iterates.
%C A398792 By Terras's theorem, the first n parities of a trajectory depend only on the residue mod 2^n, and f^(n)(2^n*m + r) = 3^s*m + f^(n)(r) where s is the number of odd steps of r; hence the counted condition holds for entire residue classes: every pair (2^n*m + r, 2^n*m + r + 1) with m >= 1 in a counted class merges, and both members have equal total stopping time whenever the merged trajectory reaches 1 (unconditionally true for all values below 2^71, see Barina).
%C A398792 a(n+1) >= 2*a(n) for all n: with m = 1 in Terras's identity, both r and r + 2^n inherit the property. Hence a(n)/2^n is nondecreasing, and every value is a lower bound for the lower density of pairs of consecutive integers with equal Collatz height: a(32)/2^32 = 0.435658..., by exact integer computation over all 2^32 residues (no sampling).
%C A398792 The class r = 4 mod 8 (n = 3) is Garner's pair (8k+4, 8k+5); the class r = 5 mod 32 (n = 5) is the family (32m+5, 32m+6) of Wu and Huang.
%C A398792 The map (3x-1)/2 yields the identical sequence for all n: negation conjugates the two maps, and the reflection r -> 2^n - 1 - r maps one counted set onto the other (machine-checked proof in Lean 4 at the link).
%C A398792 Relation to the 3x+1 problem: a(n)/2^n is a nondecreasing lower bound for the density of pairs of consecutive integers of equal Collatz height, and the classes counted at n = 3 and n = 5 are exactly the families of Garner and of Wu and Huang cited above, so the sequence gives the exact form of families that were previously known one at a time. It is not evidence for or against the Collatz conjecture: the map (3x-1)/2 gives the same values (see the comment above).
%D A398792 D. Barina, Convergence verification of the Collatz problem, J. Supercomputing 81 (2025), art. 810.
%D A398792 G.-G. Gao, On consecutive numbers of the same height in the Collatz problem, Discrete Math. 112 (1993), 261-267.
%D A398792 L. E. Garner, On heights in the Collatz 3n+1 problem, Discrete Math. 55 (1985), 57-64.
%D A398792 R. Terras, A stopping time problem on the positive integers, Acta Arith. 30 (1976), 241-252.
%D A398792 J.-B. Wu and G.-L. Huang, Family of consecutive integer pairs of the same height in the Collatz conjecture, Mathematica Applicata (Wuhan) 14 (2001), suppl., 21-25. MR 1885838.
%H A398792 Omar Said, <a href="https://github.com/theswordofthemorning/zootheorem">Statements, proofs, sequence data and verification scripts</a>
%F A398792 a(n+1) >= 2*a(n).
%e A398792 a(3) = 1: the residue r = 4 satisfies f^(3)(4) = f^(3)(5) = 2 with one odd step on each side (4 -> 2 -> 1 -> 2 and 5 -> 8 -> 4 -> 2), so every pair (8m+4, 8m+5) with m >= 1 merges in 3 steps.
%o A398792 (Python)
%o A398792 def a(n):
%o A398792     c = 0
%o A398792     for r in range(2**n):
%o A398792         x, y, sx, sy = r, r+1, 0, 0
%o A398792         for _ in range(n):
%o A398792             sx += x & 1; sy += y & 1
%o A398792             x = (3*x+1)//2 if x & 1 else x//2
%o A398792             y = (3*y+1)//2 if y & 1 else y//2
%o A398792             if x == y:
%o A398792                 c += sx == sy
%o A398792                 break
%o A398792     return c
%Y A398792 Cf. A398793 (new classes a(n+1)-2*a(n)), A398794 and A398795 (analogs for the maps (5x+1)/2 and (7x+1)/2), A076227, A100982, A006877.
%K A398792 nonn,new
%O A398792 1,4
